1
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:26,420
Yes, tea! That's my trade!

2
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:28,590
Quincy Endicott's Health Tea.

3
00:00:28,590 --> 00:00:29,920
Your tea sounds good.

4
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:31,760
Hah, never touch the stuff myself.

5
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:33,340
Bleh. Me either.

6
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:34,340
Ha ha! Yes!

7
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,340
It's all for the money, yes!

8
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:38,510
The money takes my mind off my troubles!

9
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:41,770
The deep, soul-crushing loneliness.

10
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:43,250
Yes, the more money I make,

11
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:44,920
the bigger my mansion gets,

12
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,410
the more lost I feel.

13
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:49,480
Why, this house is so big,

14
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:51,400
I sometimes don't even know where

15
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,290
or who I am!

16
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:55,110
Yeah, well, I'm glad your...

17
00:00:55,110 --> 00:00:58,840
nephews here were able to pay a visit.

18
00:00:58,840 --> 00:00:59,440
Yeah!

19
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,950
Yes, what a... what a pleasure it is to have company...

20
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:03,290
A perfect pleasure!

21
00:01:03,290 --> 00:01:04,700
A perfect pleasure, lads!

22
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:06,610
A per... yes, yes.
- Uh huh!

23
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,290
- Perfect! Perfect!
- Yeah, yeah.

24
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:09,710
♪ A most Perfect pleasure, Heather... ♪

25
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:11,030
Um, Beatrice,

26
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:12,880
w-why are you pretending I'm this guy's nephew?

27
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:14,220
We need money.

28
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:15,380
You're scamming him?

29
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:16,580
I was thinking more like

30
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:18,220
flat-out stealing from him.

31
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:19,390
What?! No way!

32
00:01:19,390 --> 00:01:20,110
Why not?

33
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:21,720
We already stole a horse.

34
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:22,470
Hey, guys.

35
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:23,470
No, we didn't.

36
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:25,890
Fred's a talking horse.
He can do whatever he wants.

37
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:27,310
I want to steal.

38
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:28,310
Huh! What?!

39
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:29,900
You guys are bonkers!

40
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:31,220
If we're going to Adelaide's,

41
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,150
- we need two cents
- Mm-hmm.

42
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,150
Well, you guys do what you...

43
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:35,990
...two cents?

44
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:38,410
- Only two cents?
- Yeah, we need two pennies

45
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:40,100
to take the ferry to Adelaide's pasture.

46
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,450
Ha ha! Come everyone!

47
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:43,800
Let us retire to the parlor and enjoy

48
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:47,250
my unnecessary excess of wealth and luxury.

49
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:50,390
Well, maybe he just has some loose change somewhere

50
00:01:50,390 --> 00:01:51,590
he wouldn't mind us taking.

51
00:01:51,590 --> 00:01:53,170
- Uh huh, mm-hm
- Mm-hm, mm-hm, mm-hm.

52
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:59,300
Behold, nephew, the majesty of wealth!

53
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:00,090
Ooh!

54
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:01,360
What was that?! Uh...

55
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,560
What's wrong unkie Endicott?
Your forehead is all sweaty.

56
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,560
M-My nerves. My...
my... my ner...

57
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:08,810
my nerves are a bust these days.

58
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:09,650
How come?

59
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,230
Uh, yes, yes. Maybe it would be...

60
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:14,730
good to talk to someone.

61
00:02:14,730 --> 00:02:15,900
Well, y...

62
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:18,480
Actually, it all began one day
when I was exploring

63
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:21,320
my exceedingly large and labyrinthine manor here.

64
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:24,330
I happened upon a section of the house I didn't even recall building.

65
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:26,000
Uh ha ha ha!
Isn't that funny?

66
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Must've been the old wing.

67
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
But it was lit in a rather sort of

68
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,630
eer... eerie ... eerie light.

69
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:32,000
And I pressed on, and then I-

70
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,200
I saw the painting of the most
beautiful woman I've ever seen,

71
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,130
and that's when...

72
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:39,650
things took a rather strange turn.

73
00:02:40,970 --> 00:02:43,910
From that day on, I was obsessed with the woman in the portrait.

74
00:02:43,910 --> 00:02:46,430
She con... consumed my every thought.

75
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:48,430
I'd fallen in love, ha ha!

76
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:49,440
...with a ghost!

77
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:51,770
Ohhhh.

78
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:53,440
It's too big. Get a grip.

79
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,440
Oh, I must sound crazy, mustn't I?

80
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,820
Perhaps it's time for you all

81
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:58,950
to leave my treasure-filled home.

82
00:02:58,950 --> 00:02:59,860
- No!
- No!

83
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:01,280
Yeah, unkie!

84
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,530
I want to see the ghost!

85
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:04,560
Ohh, how I long to see her

86
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:06,450
just once again, as well, yes.

87
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:07,540
To the painting!

88
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:09,120
Yes. Yes.

89
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:11,540
Ooh, boy! Ghost hunt!

90
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,890
Fred, go with them. (Buy me some time.)

91
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:14,960
(While I'm at it,)

92
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,050
I'll steal other stuff, too!

93
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:23,410
We're supposed to just be looking for loose change.

94
00:03:23,410 --> 00:03:24,370
That's what I'm ...

95
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:26,640
Oops. That's what I'm doing.

96
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,230
Well, let's try not
to break any more ...

97
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:30,310
Gah!

98
00:03:30,310 --> 00:03:31,730
Oh, hey.

99
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:33,480
Hey, you didn't check the armoire.

100
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,150
Tap, tap, tap...

101
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:35,150
Huh! Endicott's back

102
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:36,480
Hide!

103
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,520
Tap, tap, tap. Tap, taptaptap.

104
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:39,030
Tap.

105
00:03:41,090 --> 00:03:42,490
- Is that the portrait?
- No.

106
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:43,810
Is that the portrait?

107
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:45,780
No.
Look, w-w-why don't I-

108
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:47,860
I just tell you when we reach the portrait.

109
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:49,860
Okay, what's the matter, unkie?

110
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:51,120
You seem tense.

111
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:52,790
You know me well, nephew.

112
00:03:52,790 --> 00:03:54,490
Yes, the truth is..

113
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:56,210
I'm frightened.

114
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:57,570
Of a ghost?

115
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:59,550
Ghosts are just floaty things.

116
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:01,190
Oh, no, no, not afraid of the ghost.

117
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:03,400
I mean I'm afraid what... what... what..

118
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,090
if there is no ghost?

119
00:04:05,090 --> 00:04:09,140
What if I'm on the... on the...
on the... on the brink of...

120
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:09,850
hmm...

121
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:10,640
madness!

122
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:12,040
Maybe the doctors were right.

123
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:13,560
Come along, boys! Back to the parlor.

124
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,580
The parlor!?
What would anyone go the parlor for?

125
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:18,400
There's nobody in the parlor...

126
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,490
certainly nobody after your money.

127
00:04:21,310 --> 00:04:23,620
Yeah, no giving up now, old man.

128
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:25,410
It's got to be a ghost!

129
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:27,790
But h-how can you be so certain?

130
00:04:27,790 --> 00:04:30,710
'Cause I really, really want to see a ghost...

131
00:04:30,710 --> 00:04:31,700
- ...really bad.
- Oh.

132
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:33,660
Whew.

133
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:35,670
Okay, I think they're gone.

134
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,170
- Beatrice? You there?
- Bah! Yes, smart guy.

135
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:40,670
Start searching for change.

136
00:04:40,670 --> 00:04:43,250
Uh, I can't see anything in here.

137
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:46,220
Well, I-I don't think
these coats have pockets.

138
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:48,290
Check the lining. Maybe somebody sewed money

139
00:04:48,290 --> 00:04:49,920
- into the fabric.
- Nope.

140
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:51,550
Do people even do that?

141
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:53,120
I've done it on my clothes.

142
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:54,350
You wear clothes?

143
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:56,160
Like a little bird vest or something?

144
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:57,580
hehe, or little bows?

145
00:04:57,580 --> 00:04:59,090
When I was a human, fool!

146
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,690
You used to be human?

147
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:01,690
Did I know that?

148
00:05:01,690 --> 00:05:02,910
I-I don't think I knew that.

149
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:05,700
Jiminy Cricket! Let's just find some coins, all right?

150
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:07,700
Open the door.

151
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:09,700
It's stuck.

152
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,840
Well, guess we have to spend some quality time together.

153
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:12,500
Help!

154
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:15,020
The greenhouse.

155
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:19,920
This is where I grow my
camellia for testing new teas.

156
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,060
Perhaps we should savor the ...

157
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:24,920
the quiet tranquility of this...

158
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:26,720
this place.

159
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:30,890
Tap tap tap, tap tap.

160
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:32,310
Ah!

161
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:33,060
A ghost!

162
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:34,630
WAAAAHHH!!!

163
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:36,060
I can't do it!

164
00:05:36,310 --> 00:05:37,950
Uncle Endicott, it's
just a...

165
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:38,770
funny chicken.

166
00:05:38,770 --> 00:05:41,770
Yes, o-one of my
prize-winning roasting peacocks.

167
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:44,210
Yes, with all this love in my brain, I, uh...

168
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:46,540
forgot to feed them.
It's almost as though I've lost my...

169
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,360
mind!

170
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:51,130
We can look for that after we find our ghost

171
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:52,200
Heave-ho!

172
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:55,200
Yes, yes. Heave-ho.

173
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:56,740
Into the abyss...

174
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:58,790
never to return.

175
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:02,500
Um, anyone want
to go back to the parlor?

176
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:08,660
Hey. Are you still there?

177
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:10,510
Yes, Wirt. I'm still here.

178
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:12,860
So, how did you become a bluebird?

179
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:14,850
Hey, what's that?

180
00:06:14,850 --> 00:06:16,180
Don't change the subject.

181
00:06:16,470 --> 00:06:17,250
Hmm.

182
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:20,610
There's a breeze coming out of here.

183
00:06:21,610 --> 00:06:22,610
Oh, yeah.

184
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:24,280
- Whoa!
- What?!

185
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:25,530
A secret entrance!

186
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:26,940
So,

187
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:27,990
about your dark secret.

188
00:06:27,990 --> 00:06:31,030
Hey, how about you tell me your dark secrets instead, huh?

189
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:32,610
My secrets are too secret.

190
00:06:32,610 --> 00:06:33,950
Hey, look. Light.

191
00:06:33,950 --> 00:06:36,290
Whoa. Now who's
avoiding the question?

192
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:37,200
You.

193
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:37,960
Fine!

194
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,400
I threw a rock at a bluebird,
and it cursed me and my family,

195
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,860
and now we're all bluebirds.

196
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:43,630
Happy? Now you go.

197
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:47,050
Whoa. Your whole family?

198
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:48,050
Yeah.

199
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,210
Is that why you're going to Adelaide?

200
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:50,800
To fix things?

201
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,800
That was the plan, but...

202
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:55,220
Yeah, that was the plan.

203
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:56,570
It'll all work out.

204
00:06:56,570 --> 00:07:00,460
All I know is I am never going back till I can make them human again.

205
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:02,480
I'd do pretty much anything.

206
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:05,480
All right.
My turn, huh? Okay.

207
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:09,060
Well, it's weird to admit it, but, well, I...

208
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:10,400
I have this crush on this girl.

209
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:11,410
Mm-hmm.

210
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:12,740
That's all.

211
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:13,740
That's all?!

212
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:16,330
And I think about her a lot,
and I play clarinet.

213
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:18,410
Wirt! You got to be kidding me.

214
00:07:18,410 --> 00:07:21,080
And I secretly whisper poetry to myself in my room at night.

215
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:22,910
Wirt, that stuff's not weird.

216
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:23,920
Those are just...

217
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,540
well, the poetry thing is weird.

218
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:27,550
But those are just character traits.

219
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:29,420
- You played clarinet?
- Sort of.

220
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:31,140
What else do you do?

221
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:32,260
I don't know.

222
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:34,900
Uh... hey. Does this
room look different to you?

223
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:36,260
Uh, how so?

224
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:39,270
It's like French Rococo style.

225
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:42,440
That doesn't really seem in line with Endicott's Georgian sensibilities.

226
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:43,330
How... what?

227
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:45,020
Who on Earth am I talking to right now?

228
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:46,640
Should I not know that sort of stuff?

229
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:49,110
Endicott probably just built
it without thinking, right?

230
00:07:49,110 --> 00:07:51,580
Or... whoa! Yeah.

231
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:54,620
W-What if the ghost he was talking about was actually...

232
00:07:58,450 --> 00:07:59,730
This is the room where...

233
00:07:59,730 --> 00:08:01,730
the room where I first saw her.

234
00:08:03,030 --> 00:08:05,570
Whooooooa...

235
00:08:05,570 --> 00:08:07,460
Just a bedroom?

236
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:09,700
Just a bedroom, my nephew?

237
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:11,630
This is the chamber of mine own

238
00:08:11,630 --> 00:08:13,630
true love, and here she stands,

239
00:08:13,630 --> 00:08:15,630
hovering above us

240
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:18,310
like the blinding sun.

241
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:21,230
Hmm. No ghost?

242
00:08:21,230 --> 00:08:23,960
No ghost?! Then I am...

243
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:24,650
mad.

244
00:08:24,650 --> 00:08:25,940
Don't be mad.

245
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:26,900
Heeeey.

246
00:08:26,900 --> 00:08:29,570
What's this suspicious mess over here?

247
00:08:29,570 --> 00:08:31,380
It looks like there was a struggle...

248
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:33,820
a violent struggle.

249
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:38,240
What are you implying,
my equine friend?

250
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:40,160
Nothing, nothing. I'm just...

251
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:43,010
Endicott is a crazed lunatic who did away with the lady of the house

252
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:44,710
and is now pretending to own the place!

253
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:45,250
What?!

254
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,520
Who do you think you are, making accusa...

255
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:48,380
I see it now.

256
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,380
You're after my money.

257
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:52,670
Do you the things that I did for this money?

258
00:08:52,670 --> 00:08:57,300
T-The things these filthy hands have done to make this money?!

259
00:08:57,300 --> 00:08:59,310
I'll never steal again,

260
00:08:59,310 --> 00:09:00,470
I swear. I'll get an honest job.

261
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:02,640
And what say you... nephew?

262
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:05,480
Uh, I'm confused.

263
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:06,480
Well, then...

264
00:09:06,250 --> 00:09:11,030
OoOoOoOoOo...

265
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:10,980
She comes for me!

266
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:11,980
Wait, unkie!

267
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,150
Face your fears!

268
00:09:13,150 --> 00:09:13,820
Huh!

269
00:09:18,070 --> 00:09:20,370
Mr. Endicott? Are you all right?

270
00:09:20,370 --> 00:09:22,330
Psst! Ghost.

271
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:25,830
What do you want with me, spirit?

272
00:09:25,830 --> 00:09:28,170
Spirit? But you're the ghost.

273
00:09:28,170 --> 00:09:32,170
My lady, I assure you
I am flesh and blood and I...

274
00:09:32,170 --> 00:09:33,920
I welcome you to my home.

275
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:36,310
Your home? Good sir,

276
00:09:36,310 --> 00:09:37,930
you're in my home.

277
00:09:37,930 --> 00:09:38,930
Impossible!

278
00:09:39,930 --> 00:09:40,930
Look here.

279
00:09:40,930 --> 00:09:41,930
You see?

280
00:09:41,930 --> 00:09:44,390
This is my camellia garden

281
00:09:44,390 --> 00:09:46,390
for my tea company.

282
00:09:46,390 --> 00:09:48,060
Margueritte Grey?!

283
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:49,980
Why, with all due respect, madam,

284
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:51,730
this is my tea garden.

285
00:09:51,730 --> 00:09:53,730
Quincy Endicott?!

286
00:09:53,890 --> 00:09:56,540
Your guys' mansions are so huge

287
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:57,570
they're actually connected.

288
00:09:57,570 --> 00:10:00,750
So you... you mean that beautiful ghost

289
00:10:00,750 --> 00:10:02,080
was really just...

290
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:04,820
That dashing specter was really just...

291
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:07,410
- My business competitor?
- My business competitor?

292
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:10,840
Well, Greg, my boy,

293
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:14,590
I can't thank you enough for helping me to face my fears.

294
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:17,760
You're a... you're a sweet boy with good sense.

295
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:19,430
Take this penny...

296
00:10:19,430 --> 00:10:21,260
and start your fortune.

297
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:23,930
And here's one from me, as well.

298
00:10:23,930 --> 00:10:26,770
Hey, nice.
Now we can ride the ferry.

299
00:10:26,770 --> 00:10:27,940
Well, everybody,

300
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:29,240
I think it's time we head to

301
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:31,940
Adelaide of the pasture,
the good woman of the woods.

302
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:34,190
Uh, what about you, Fred?
Are you coming with us?

303
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:36,560
No, thanks. I've got a real job now

304
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:38,610
as an official tea horse.

305
00:10:38,610 --> 00:10:39,950
Well, then I guess that's it.

306
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:41,870
Off you go, the lot of you.

307
00:10:41,870 --> 00:10:43,140
Goodbye.

308
00:10:42,490 --> 00:10:43,870
- Goodbye!
- Goodbye.

309
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:43,870
Goodbye.

310
00:10:43,870 --> 00:10:46,870
And don't forget to
buy Endicott brand tea.

311
00:10:48,790 --> 00:10:50,790
Hey, Wirt.
You did good back there.

312
00:10:50,790 --> 00:10:51,380
Yeah?

313
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:53,630
You had a lot more going for you than I thought.

314
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:55,210
Huh?

315
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:01,980
Greg! Our pennies!

316
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:03,050
Why did you do that?!

317
00:11:03,050 --> 00:11:05,590
'Cause uncle Endicott
pegged me all wrong ...

318
00:11:05,590 --> 00:11:09,060
I've got no "cents,"
no "cents" at all.
